"""Additional explicit finite illustrations for source batch 03."""
import math
from math_ops import op

op('binomtail','N P Cut','Tail','[Array.from({length:p[0]+1},(_,k)=>k).filter(k=>k>p[2]).reduce((s,k)=>{let c=1;for(let j=1;j<=k;j++)c=c*(p[0]-j+1)/j;return s+c*Math.pow(p[1],k)*Math.pow(1-p[1],p[0]-k);},0)]',lambda p:[sum(math.comb(int(p[0]),k)*p[1]**k*(1-p[1])**(p[0]-k) for k in range(int(p[0])+1) if k>p[2])],'For {N} independent toy births with boy probability {P}, what is the probability of strictly more than {Cut} boys?','The binomial calculation counts every qualifying outcome. Smaller samples have more variable proportions; equal expected proportions do not mean equal tail probabilities.','grid')
op('samplesd','P N','SD','[Math.sqrt(p[0]*(1-p[0])/p[1])]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(p[0]*(1-p[0])/p[1])],'For {N} independent toy Bernoulli observations with probability {P}, what is the standard deviation of the sample proportion?','Sampling spread depends on sample size as well as the underlying probability. This exact model assumes independent observations.','grid')
op('weightprob','P Alpha','Weight','[p[0]===0?0:p[0]===1?1:Math.exp(-Math.pow(-Math.log(p[0]),p[1]))]',lambda p:[0 if p[0]==0 else 1 if p[0]==1 else math.exp(-(-math.log(p[0]))**p[1])],'A toy probability-weighting rule is exp(-(-log(P))^Alpha). With P {P} and Alpha {Alpha}, what decision weight results?','A decision weight differs from an event frequency. The specified curve is a model assumption and cannot establish what every person believes.','curve')
op('commonpart','Common Gain Sure P','Advantage','[(p[0]+p[3]*p[1])-(p[0]+p[2])]',lambda p:[p[3]*p[1]-p[2]],'A toy pair shares payoff {Common}. One adds gain {Gain} with probability {P}; the other adds sure {Sure}. What is risky minus sure expected cash?','A truly common component cancels from this expected-cash comparison. Preferences over lotteries require an additional utility model.')
op('ambigbounds','Prize Low High','Lower Upper','[p[0]*p[1],p[0]*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1],p[0]*p[2]],'A toy prize is {Prize}; its unknown win probability lies between {Low} and {High}. What is the lower bound on expected winnings?','An interval of plausible probabilities yields bounds, rather than a uniquely identified expectation. It does not specify anyone’s attitude toward ambiguity.','grid')
op('logutility','Wealth Gain Loss P','Change','[p[3]*Math.log(p[0]+p[1])+(1-p[3])*Math.log(p[0]-p[2])-Math.log(p[0])]',lambda p:[p[3]*math.log(p[0]+p[1])+(1-p[3])*math.log(p[0]-p[2])-math.log(p[0])],'A toy log-utility agent has wealth {Wealth}, gains {Gain} with probability {P} and otherwise loses {Loss}. What is expected log utility minus log current wealth?','Curvature, initial wealth and stakes jointly determine this model comparison. Log utility is an assumed rule, not a universal account of risk preferences.')
op('portfoliosd','Weight SD1 SD2 Correlation','SD','[Math.sqrt(p[0]*p[0]*p[1]*p[1]+Math.pow(1-p[0],2)*p[2]*p[2]+2*p[0]*(1-p[0])*p[1]*p[2]*p[3])]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(p[0]**2*p[1]**2+(1-p[0])**2*p[2]**2+2*p[0]*(1-p[0])*p[1]*p[2]*p[3])],'A toy portfolio puts weight {Weight} in an asset with SD {SD1}, the rest in one with SD {SD2}; correlation {Correlation}. What is portfolio SD?','Covariance contributes to portfolio risk. Equal component risks do not imply equal portfolio risks when correlations or weights differ.')
op('digitgap','PriceA PriceB','CashGap IntegerGap','[p[1]-p[0],Math.floor(p[1])-Math.floor(p[0])]',lambda p:[p[1]-p[0],math.floor(p[1])-math.floor(p[0])],'Toy prices are {PriceA} and {PriceB}. What is the cash difference, PriceB minus PriceA?','The cash gap and the change in the integer part are separate quantities. This display does not measure how large the difference feels.','grid')
op('profit','Quantity Price Cost Charity','Profit','[p[0]*(p[1]*(1-p[3])-p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]*(p[1]*(1-p[3])-p[2])],'A toy sale has {Quantity} units at price {Price}, unit cost {Cost} and fraction {Charity} of revenue donated. What profit remains?','Quantity, price, costs and transfers all enter profit. Sales alone cannot establish the profitability of a pricing policy.')
op('unitprice','Price Quantity Units','UnitCost','[p[0]*p[2]/p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[2]/p[1]],'A toy pack costs {Price} for {Quantity} units. What is its price per {Units} units?','A common unit denominator makes unequal packages comparable. Package appearance does not change their stated contents.','grid')
op('flatrate','Fixed PerUse Uses','Extra','[p[0]-p[1]*p[2]]',lambda p:[p[0]-p[1]*p[2]],'A toy flat plan costs {Fixed}; usage pricing costs {PerUse} for each of {Uses} uses. How much more does the flat plan cost?','Compare plans at the same usage level. Uncertainty about future use would be a separate input.')
op('carry','Debt Interest Cash Yield','Drag','[p[0]*p[1]-p[2]*p[3]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]-p[2]*p[3]],'Toy debt {Debt} costs interest {Interest}; cash {Cash} earns yield {Yield} over the same period. What is interest cost minus cash income?','Use the same horizon for both sides of the balance sheet. The arithmetic excludes liquidity needs and transaction costs.')
op('raise','Salary Initial Raise Allocated Years','SavedRate Salary','[(p[0]*p[1]+p[3]*(p[0]*Math.pow(1+p[2],p[4])-p[0]))/(p[0]*Math.pow(1+p[2],p[4])),p[0]*Math.pow(1+p[2],p[4])]',lambda p:[(p[0]*p[1]+p[3]*(p[0]*(1+p[2])**p[4]-p[0]))/(p[0]*(1+p[2])**p[4]),p[0]*(1+p[2])**p[4]],'Toy salary {Salary} grows by {Raise} each year for {Years} years. Initial savings are fraction {Initial} of initial salary; fraction {Allocated} of all raises is added to annual saving. What is the final saving rate?','Committing part of future raises changes the saving rate without requiring the same immediate cut to current consumption. The salary path is assumed.','grid')
op('fees','Principal Return Fee Years','Balance','[p[0]*Math.pow(1+p[1]-p[2],p[3])]',lambda p:[p[0]*(1+p[1]-p[2])**p[3]],'Toy principal {Principal} grows at annual return {Return} minus annual fee {Fee} for {Years} years. What balance results?','Repeated fees compound through the balance. The constant return is an illustrative assumption and excludes taxes and uncertainty.')
op('rsquared','R','Percent','[100*p[0]*p[0]]',lambda p:[100*p[0]**2],'In a toy simple linear regression with an intercept and correlation R {R}, what percent of outcome variation is R squared?','Squaring a correlation gives the explained share in this simple linear setting. It is neither a causal effect nor an individual prediction guarantee.','grid')
op('peakend','Peak End Mean Duration','Memory Total','[(p[0]+p[1])/2,p[2]*p[3]]',lambda p:[(p[0]+p[1])/2,p[2]*p[3]],'A toy memory rule averages peak {Peak} and end {End}. The actual experience has mean {Mean} and duration {Duration}. What is the memory-rule score?','The peak-end summary and the time-integrated total answer different questions. Changing duration alone changes total exposure while leaving this assumed summary fixed.','grid')
op('adapt','Baseline Shock Decay Time','Level','[p[0]+p[1]*Math.exp(-p[2]*p[3])]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[1]*math.exp(-p[2]*p[3])],'Toy level is baseline {Baseline} plus shock {Shock} times exp(-Decay {Decay} × Time {Time}). What level results?','An assumed fading shock produces adaptation toward the baseline. Real wellbeing need not follow this curve or recover fully.','grid')
op('selectedpool','Visible VisibleN Missing MissingN','Pooled','[(p[0]*p[1]+p[2]*p[3])/(p[1]+p[3])]',lambda p:[(p[0]*p[1]+p[2]*p[3])/(p[1]+p[3])],'Toy visible studies average {Visible} across {VisibleN} equal-weight studies; missing studies average {Missing} across {MissingN}. What is the mean if all are included?','Selection of studies can change a pooled estimate. Missing results here are explicit sensitivity assumptions, not recovered observations.','grid')
op('removeinvalid','Sum N RemovedSum RemovedN','RemainingMean','[(p[0]-p[2])/(p[1]-p[3])]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[2])/(p[1]-p[3])],'A toy dataset totals {Sum} over {N} records. Exclude {RemovedN} invalid records totaling {RemovedSum}. What is the remaining mean?','Invalid data cannot be retained as evidence merely because they support a preferred story. This arithmetic illustrates exclusion, not a reconstruction of a retracted study.','grid')
op('outlier','Regular N Extreme','Mean Median','[(p[0]*p[1]+p[2])/(p[1]+1),p[0]]',lambda p:[(p[0]*p[1]+p[2])/(p[1]+1),p[0]],'A toy sample has {N} identical values {Regular} and one extreme {Extreme}. What is the sample mean?','The extreme value can move the mean substantially while the median of this sample stays at the repeated value. Sensitivity does not itself establish which observations are invalid.','grid')
